#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Homogeneous BVPs"}] :question/id #uuid "b40412bb-d2d7-519e-aad6-60f53f4a1fcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21797" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0, \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad \\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$.\n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}y_{1}(a) + β_{1}y_{1}^{′}(a) & α_{1}y_{2}(a) + β_{1}y_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ α_{2}y_{1}(b) + β_{2}y_{1}^{′}(b) & α_{2}y_{2}(b) + β_{2}y_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting $\\alpha_i=0$, $\\beta_i=1$, $a=1$, and $b=3$, the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}$. The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} - 2m - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(m - 3)(m + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1$, and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3(1)} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{3(3)} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{9} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= (3e^{3})(-e^{-3}) - (-e^{-1})(3e^{9}) \\\\\n&=-3 + 3e^{8} \\\\\n&= 3 (e 8 - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228888 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4607104f-d91b-50b8-a446-377eb37de5a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Homogeneous BVPs With Nontrivial Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "b6c06f26-9ff1-5e60-b0e3-5ee7482dff34" :question/math-academy-id "e-21799" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y'' = 0, \\qquad y(4) = 0, \\quad y(3) + y'(3) = 0,\\quad 3\\leq x \\leq 4$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A$." :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n$y''(x) = 0$.\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n$y' = A$.\n\nApplying the condition $y(4) = 0$, we have\n\n$$\n4A + B = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad B = -4A\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ax - 4A = A(x-4)\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(3) + y'(3) = 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n-A+A = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cdot \\left(x-4\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Homogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88efc39b-d3bc-53bd-9338-badaae985e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}(x) = \\sin 3x] and [MATH: y_{2}(x) = \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228421} {:answer/id #uuid "aa770812-53d7-5a63-9631-64d1726f5089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}(x) = e^{3x}] and [MATH: y_{2}(x) = e^{− x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228422} {:answer/id #uuid "ac54d522-f38d-576d-9a0e-6c66b8756743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}(x) = \\sin 2x] and [MATH: y_{2}(x) = \\cos 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228423} {:answer/id #uuid "52985bcf-ca88-57ac-8f05-453ccf8e7395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}(x) = e^{2x}] and [MATH: y_{2}(x) = e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228424}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7576ea93-e8c1-5d88-8072-8b71322bee65" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228420} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e6bc2b-7da1-5428-a625-a9092c510f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 − e^{4})/(e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228426} {:answer/id #uuid "c1087e67-5046-51c5-9380-ce4f875af4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{2} + e^{4})/(e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228427} {:answer/id #uuid "38e4ca5a-4e04-575c-b5af-ec9d43fa38c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228428} {:answer/id #uuid "52b0ba27-8a59-510f-b207-4a22e48b75ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228429} {:answer/id #uuid "761aea0c-1d32-59f3-95f5-a2640a4b4f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 − e^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228430}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6f9ebf19-22b0-5923-b28b-a1c31951131b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce4981bf-def3-5b7b-b715-20fcecf0e522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228432} {:answer/id #uuid "322c202d-1949-5091-a1ab-2cf4b3b46ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228433} {:answer/id #uuid "56a13b61-2015-5809-9122-92f24c3a4fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228434}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5b690f-d357-53b4-bc5c-ce9f9fd6d5f9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3ef8ea1-dd48-59b6-98ca-0b4b4df69ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A) ≠ 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228436} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd473dd-10d8-5658-aaa1-12d5a8b9d7fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228437}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd62d9d-8d0d-5e25-9e73-725a5394529f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228435}] :question/id #uuid "cdee9cef-7184-5408-a29d-c97a87168d80" :question/math-academy-id "q-338525" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″} - 2y^{′} - 3y = 0, y(0) = y(1) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 1$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}(0) & y_{2}(0) \\\\ y_{1}(1) & y_{2}(1)\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\nThe fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\nFor this BVP, we have $det(A) =$ {{field-3}}.\n\nTherefore, this BVP {{field-4}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4607104f-d91b-50b8-a446-377eb37de5a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Homogeneous BVPs With Nontrivial Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "57812598-2970-5fd9-93fd-7f337c6d99d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228457}] :question/id #uuid "382e2b54-213f-5d6d-8b77-7c5a76835556" :question/math-academy-id "q-338544" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y^{″} = 0, y(-2) = 0, y(2) - 4y^{′}(2) = 0,-2 \\le x \\le 2$\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A$.\n\nThe solution is $y = A \\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)$."}]}
